PRETABLE -- #530 (lcaisalane) kill table 1.  Written BEFORE the table runs; this file's mtime proves the order.
Instrument: regress/lca_isalane_probe.py, 37 rows (16 witness rows, sentinel re-pinned 0x0bad1dea -> 0x0bad1d8a,
+ 21 detector rows), frozen by the driver.  Baseline: the #530 working set (src/devices/dev_lca.c, disposition B per
../FABLE_F2_RULING_20260914.md).  Measured before this file: HEAD_repin.out 13/16 (same three reds as 2026-09-10),
HEAD_det.out 19/37, FIX_det.out 37/37 (build/, two-part clearance CLEAN/FRESH on all three build trees).

1. SIBLING PARITY.  The function has two arms (read, write) and the fix makes them use ONE position rule.  Every cell
   the fix changes is rowed on BOTH arms where a guest can drive it: (1,1) r11/w11, (2,2) r22/w22, (1,2) r12/w12,
   (1,3) r13/w13, (2,3) r23/w23; the unchanged (2,1) on both (r21/w21) as the anchor.  (3,3) and len 5-7 heads need a
   1 KB-split TRANSLATED access this probe cannot build on K0SEG -- DISCLOSED (e), as the ruling says.
2. CLAIM -> ROW.  The added comment asserts: position (P & 3) % len on both arms (r11 r21 r81 r22 r12 r13 r23 w11 w21
   w22 w12 w13 w23); the old exit ended the host (six `alive' rows + witness ofs2/ofs3 exit); the read arm discarded
   the byte at ofs == len (r11 r22 + witness ofs1 zero); one byte forwarded whatever len (no row: F7 / lcaisasize owns
   width, DISCLOSED); len 0 refused before the % (no producer -- m_lenzero predicted EQUIVALENT, DISCLOSED); the
   unaligned-LDL producer (no row: split needs a translated access -- DISCLOSED, lcaisaunaligned filed).
3. GUARD FIELD vs BODY FIELD.  The fix's only guard reads `len'; the body reads `len', `relative_addr', `data[]',
   `writeflag'.  `len' and `relative_addr' are caller arguments written at one site (the dispatch), never device state
   -- no disagreeing-state row owed.  N/A with that reason.
4. CHANNEL (per row: mutant killed / observable that differs).
   r11 byte      m_decleq (sentinel kept), m_writeonlyfold (0), m_retzero (sentinel)  -- FULL register vs flat LSR
   r21 anchor    m_replicate (0x6060), m_retzero                                     -- full register
   r81 lane      m_nomask (byte at position 5), m_replicate, m_retzero                -- full register
   r22 byte      m_decleq, m_writeonlyfold, m_replicate (0xb0b0), m_retzero           -- full register vs flat MSR
   w11 lands     m_decleq (store dropped), m_readonlyfold (data[1] past a 1-byte buffer) -- flat IER readback
   w21 lane      none in this table (unchanged cell); anchor for w23                   -- flat IER readback
   w22 lands     m_decleq, m_readonlyfold                                            -- flat MSR readback
   <tag> alive   m_exit (the reinstated exit ends exactly that process)               -- process liveness
   r12/r13 byte  m_exit, m_declbeyond, m_decleq (sentinel), m_writeonlyfold (0), m_retzero -- full register
   r23 lane      + m_clamp (0x5a at position 0), m_replicate (0x5a5a)                 -- full register
   w12/w13 lands m_exit, m_declbeyond, m_decleq (dropped), m_readonlyfold             -- flat readback
   w13 roundtrip + m_writeonlyfold (0), m_retzero (sentinel)                          -- full register, SAME sparse addr
   w23 lane      + m_clamp (data[0] = 0x00)                                          -- flat readback
   witness rows: ofs1 zero (m_decleq -- NOW red because the re-pinned sentinel has bits 5-6 clear; m_writeonlyfold,
   m_retzero), ofs1 answer (m_decleq, m_retzero), ofs1/2/3 wide (m_lane1 at 2/3, m_retzero), ofs2/3 exit (m_exit),
   live + ctl zero (m_retzero).
   CHECK OF THE CHANNEL LINE ITSELF (the carrier asks the first applications to check it, not obey it): every channel
   above was chosen at writing time from the source alone; the table below says whether each held.

